{
 "sub_strand": "MATH.G8.NUMBER.DECIMALS",
 "title": "Decimals",
 "status": "draft: written 18 Nov 2026 offline for Grade 8; not yet checked by a Kenyan maths teacher; not loaded by the live tutor",
 "sources": [],
 "notes": "Official outcomes a to h are tested; i (use IT) and j (promote use) are not questions. Recurring decimals are written with dots over the repeating digits on paper; on a phone the families write 0.2727... . Recurring decimals to fractions (c) take one or two repeating digits from the first decimal place (0.3..., 0.27...); a repeat that starts later (0.16...) is left for a teacher to confirm. Significant figures (e) count from the first non-zero digit; zeros that only hold the place are kept in a whole number (48,000 to 2 s.f.). Standard form (f) is A × 10 to a power with A from 1 up to but not including 10, for large and small numbers; one family multiplies two numbers in standard form, which goes a little beyond the outcome (teacher to confirm).",
 "skills": [
  {
